desc:
The four kinds of clinging — clinging to sense desire, clinging to views, clinging to rules and vows, and clinging to the doctrine of self — are the intensification and hardening of “craving.”

- Clinging to sense desire: grasping at the five strands of sense desire, the hardening of craving at the sensory level;
- Clinging to views: grasping at various opinions and theories, holding that “only this is true and all else is false”;
- Clinging to rules and vows: taking useless austerities, precepts, or taboos as the cause of liberation, such as clinging to the cow vow or the dog vow;
- Clinging to the doctrine of self: grasping at the various statements concerning “self,” the most fundamental of the four clingings.

The classificatory significance of the four clingings is that they extend the clinging of [[samsara|saṃsāra]] from desire to theory and self-identification — erudition and austerity can equally become the bonds of saṃsāra.

Action and volitional activity, divided into the three karmas of body, speech, and mind. Karma is the concrete unfolding of [[dependent_origination|dependent origination]] at the ethical level, and its content comprises four defining points — these are the key to understanding the Buddhist view of karmic fruition:

(1) It is not divine punishment: there is no judge, and karmic fruition is determined by the conditions themselves;
(2) It is not a mechanical one-to-one correspondence: the same action yields fruits of differing weight according to the state of mind, the object, and the occasion;
(3) Not everything is predestined: there is both definite and indefinite karma, and practice can alter the time and the weight of the fruition;
(4) It is not isolated: there is both shared karma and individual karma, and the karma of the individual and that of the world in which that individual lives interweave.

[[karma_types|The classification of karma]]: definite and indefinite karma (with respect to its force); fruition in this life, in the next life, and in a later life (with respect to the time of fruition); shared and individual karma (with respect to the scope of the agents). There is in addition the distinction between “wholesome with outflows” (which binds one to birth-and-death) and “wholesome without outflows” (which leads to liberation). The treatises take “volition” as the substance of karma — “volition itself is karma” — so that the essence of karma lies in the will and not in the outward act.

The maturing of karmic fruition. Cause and effect ripen “at a different time, of a different kind, and with transformation,” whence the name “ripening”:
- Different time: cause and effect do not occur at the same moment;
- Different kind: the cause is wholesome or unwholesome, whereas the fruit itself is “indeterminate” (it cannot simply be called wholesome or unwholesome);
- Transformation: the fruit changes in the course of ripening.

These three senses are precisely what address the difficulty that “the one who acts and the one who receives the fruit are not the same entity”: the fruit is not a copy of the cause but a matured result, at a different time, of a different nature, and having undergone change.

Continuation. The image of the “wheel” is taken from a wheel's revolving without beginning or end, going round and round. Buddhism speaks of saṃsāra, but the crucial point is this: within saṃsāra there is no fixed “soul” that undergoes rebirth.

This is not a compromising formulation but the core proposition of the Buddhist view of saṃsāra. It unfolds into four points:
- The [[continuity|continuity]] of the consciousness aggregate — the arising and passing away of consciousness is unceasing;
- The continuity of karmic force — the operation of karma does not vanish with the body;
- The continuity of craving — [[craving|craving]] ceaselessly produces new existence;
- The continuity of the five aggregates — the aggregation of the five aggregates is ceaselessly reconstituted.

As a flame passing to a lamp, lamp continuing from lamp — not the same fire, yet not unrelated. Saṃsāra is therefore “continuity” and not “the relocation of a substance,” and this is also the way the proposition “rebirth without a self” is answered.

A polysemous term with at least six senses:
(1) Teaching — the doctrine spoken by the Buddha;
(2) Truth, law — dharma-nature, dharma-realm;
(3) All existence, phenomena — “all dharmas”;
(4) The object of mind-consciousness — the “dharma object” opposed to the mind faculty;
(5) Wholesome, unwholesome, and indeterminate dharmas — dharma in the classification of value;
(6) The “dharma-substance” of the Abhidharma — the own-nature posited by the [[sarvastivada|Sarvāstivāda]].

In the context of “[[conditioned|conditioned dharmas]] / unconditioned dharmas” the third sense is taken, referring to all existence and phenomena. This multiplicity of levels is a precondition for understanding Buddhist literature: in the single sentence “all dharmas are without self,” the scope of “dharma” differs according to context, and the judgment varies accordingly — for example, if “all dharmas are without self” includes unconditioned dharmas, its conclusion differs from the reading in which “without self” refers only to conditioned dharmas.

The original meaning of “dharma” is related to “to hold” (dhṛ): what holds its own characteristic is called a dharma. This etymology shows that the Buddhist understanding of “existence” takes the “own-characteristic” as its unit rather than “substance.”

Not fashioned by causes and conditions, without arising or ceasing. It usually refers to [[nirvana|nirvāṇa]], [[suchness|suchness]], analytical cessation, space, and so forth.

The schools differ in their views on the number and the reality of unconditioned dharmas:
- The Sarvāstivāda posits three unconditioned dharmas (analytical cessation, non-analytical cessation, and space) and admits unconditioned dharmas as really existent;
- The Yogācāra posits six unconditioned dharmas (space, analytical cessation, non-analytical cessation, imperturbability, the cessation of perception and feeling, and suchness), taking suchness as the perfected nature;
- The Madhyamaka does not hold unconditioned dharmas to be really existent, saying that the conditioned, just as it is, is empty and that this itself is the unconditioned;
- The Tiantai, Huayan, and Chan, on the other hand, reveal the unconditioned precisely in and through the conditioned, and do not seek the unconditioned apart from phenomena.

These differences are not disagreements over detail but the point at which each school's fundamental position on “whether truth exists independently of phenomena” lands.

Form, feeling, perception, formations, and consciousness. They are used to analyse the constitution of the “person” without presupposing a substantial self.

- Form: material existence, the four great elements and the matter derived from them;
- Feeling: the feelings of suffering, pleasure, and equanimity;
- Perception: the apprehending of characteristics, cognitive marking;
- Formations: all mental factors other than feeling and perception, especially volitional activity;
- Consciousness: the function of discernment.

The five aggregates are “analytical”: they break experience into five classes of process, among which no class can be found that matches a “self” that is “permanent, single, and sovereign.” The form of argument in the sūtras is: form is impermanent → the impermanent is suffering → the suffering is not self → what is not self is also not what belongs to a self; and likewise for feeling, perception, formations, and consciousness.

The five aggregates, the [[twelve_ayatana|twelve sense bases]], and the [[eighteen_dhatu|eighteen elements]] constitute three complementary angles of analysis: the five aggregates lay weight on the constitution of the “person” (material and mental factors), the twelve sense bases on the constitution of “cognition” (faculties and objects), and the eighteen elements comprise both and add the corresponding consciousness. One and the same body of experience, cut three ways, with the same purpose — to find no “self” in the analysis.

Posited by the [[sarvastivada|Sarvāstivāda]]: the unconditioned of analytical cessation, the unconditioned of non-analytical cessation, and the unconditioned of space, admitting [[unconditioned|unconditioned dharmas]] as really existent.

- The unconditioned of analytical cessation: the cessation attained by extinguishing affliction through “analysis” (the discerning power of wisdom);
- The unconditioned of non-analytical cessation: the cessation obtained not through the discernment of wisdom but because, the conditions being absent, the dharma does not arise;
- The unconditioned of space: non-obstruction, not obstructed by material dharmas.

The claim that the three unconditioned dharmas are “really existent” is part of the Sarvāstivāda position that “the three times really exist and the substance of dharmas is eternally existent,” and it therefore became a target of criticism for the Madhyamaka and the Sautrāntika.

The real condition of dharmas, their original face. “True” (zhen) means real, “thus” (ru) means unchanging constancy — “suchness” is “as it originally is.”

Its meaning shifts markedly among different systems:
- The [[vijnapti_matra|Yogācāra]] takes suchness as the [[parinispanna|perfected nature]] — revealed on the dependent nature by departing from pervasive grasping, and belonging to “principle”;
- The Madhyamaka takes suchness as another name for emptiness — positing no separate entity;
- The tathāgatagarbha lineage takes “suchness within affliction” as the tathāgatagarbha and “suchness released from affliction” as the dharmakāya (*Foxing lun*), so that suchness becomes the common ground of delusion and awakening;
- The *Awakening of Faith in the Mahāyāna* (*Dasheng qixin lun*) takes “one mind” as “the great totality of the one dharma-realm,” gathering suchness, dharma-realm, dependent origination, emptiness of own-nature, and the dependent nature into a single place, and so becomes the teaching of “suchness as dependent origination.”

The “seven kinds of suchness” of the *Saṃdhinirmocana-sūtra* (Jie Shenmi Jing) are the key point in the systematization of this concept: from then on suchness became the common ground of all continuation and reversion.

The vertical classification of the dwelling places of sentient beings in Buddhism, and also the spatial unfolding of the concept of “[[samsara|saṃsāra]]”:

- **The realm of desire** — possessing sexual desire and material food, including the hells, hungry ghosts, animals, humans, asuras, and the **six heavens of desire** (from the heaven of the Four Great Kings to the heaven that enjoys the emanations of others);
- **The form realm** — having transcended desire yet still possessing shape and colour, namely the **seventeen heavens of the four dhyānas** (three heavens of the first dhyāna, three of the second, three of the third, and eight of the fourth, among which the five pure abodes are the dwelling of noble ones);
- **The formless realm** — without shape or colour, only feeling, perception, formations, and consciousness, namely the **four formless heavens** (the sphere of infinite space, the sphere of infinite consciousness, the sphere of nothingness, and the sphere of neither-perception-nor-non-perception).

The twenty-eight heavens are the seventeen heavens of the form realm and the four heavens of the formless realm plus the six heavens of the realm of desire, a further reckoning that adds the destinies below the human realm; the number differs among the schools (the names and numbers of the heavens differ between the [[sarvastivada|Sarvāstivāda]] and the Theravāda), and this page adopts no single account.

**Why this layer is needed** First, it explains the place of fruition of the “four dhyānas and eight concentrations”: the fruition produced by cultivating absorption lies precisely in the form realm and the formless realm, so that **meditative absorption is not liberation** — one may be born in the heaven of neither-perception-nor-non-perception with a lifespan of eighty thousand great kalpas, and when the fruition is exhausted one still falls. Second, it marks out the gradient of “rebirth without a self”: from the hells to the formless heavens, it is the unfolding of one and the same mechanism of karmic force under different conditions, and there is no place that is a resting place for a “self.” Third, the Chinese and Southern traditions record different numbers, names, and lifespans of the heavens, and one must note which treatise a Buddhist text follows.

A hundred years after the Buddha's parinirvāṇa the saṅgha gradually divided over details of [[precepts|the precepts]] and over differences in the understanding of the teaching: first into the “two root schools” — the **[[southern_tradition|Sthaviras]]** (Sthavira, weighting the Vinaya and the treatises and taking the elders as the norm) and the **Mahāsāṃghikas** (Mahāsāṃghika, weighting the sūtras and expedient means and taking an open attitude to the teaching) — and thereafter into further subdivisions, recorded by tradition as “eighteen schools” or “twenty schools.”

The causes of the division were not only doctrinal but still more a matter of **Vinaya practice and saṅgha polity** (whether the ten points were legitimate, whether gold and silver might be accepted, and so on); a considerable part of the doctrinal differences was acknowledged only after the fact. A table of the schools should therefore not be read backwards from its outcome and taken as a purely intellectual genealogy.

The schools that mattered most for the history of theory are these:

- **The Sarvāstivāda** (Sarvāstivāda) — holding the reality of the three times and the five categories and seventy-five dharmas, with the most complete system of treatises;
- **The Sautrāntika** (Sautrāntika) — weighting the sūtras and slighting the treatises, holding that past and future have no substance and only the present is real, and criticising the Sarvāstivāda continuously;
- **The Vātsīputrīya** (Vātsīputrīya) — positing an “inexpressible pudgala,” giving a substantialist answer between non-self and the fruit of karma, and judged by the other schools to be “a heterodox sect attached to the Buddha's Dharma”;
- **The Mahāsāṃghikas** — more open in their understanding of the Buddha-body and the bodhisattva path, and regarded as one of the proximate sources of Mahāyāna thought (this view is contested in scholarship and is not settled).

The positive significance of the division of the schools is often underestimated: it was precisely recitation and debate carried on separately that produced multiple recensions of one and the same scripture — the differences between the four Chinese Āgamas and the four Pali Nikāyas are just the product of the schools' transmission.

“Abhidharma” means “towards the Dharma” or “the higher Dharma” — the discernment that faces towards [[nirvana|nirvāṇa]], the analysis of the own-characteristic of each dharma. It is not a class of content but a **self-awareness of method**: the teaching scattered through the sūtras is reorganised into a body of knowledge with precise classifications, clear definitions, and no mutual contradiction.

The method of the treatises has certain fixed features, by which any treatise may be recognised:

1. **Classification and definition** — the terms are first listed, then defined (as “form means being subject to change and destruction”);
2. **Own-characteristic and shared characteristic** — each dharma has its own-characteristic (which makes it a dharma), and dharmas have characteristics in common (impermanence, suffering, [[anatta|non-self]]);
3. **Catechetical form** — the boundaries of a definition are forced out by objections and replies;
4. **The gates of inclusion, association, cause, and result** — the same set of dharmas is reorganised from different angles to test whether the classification is exhaustive and non-overlapping.

The formation of Abhidharma is itself the history of the schools: the Sthavira lineage developed the seven Pali treatises and the *Visuddhimagga*; the Sarvāstivāda developed the “six feet and one body” — the *Six Feet* treatises together with the *Jñānaprasthāna* were gathered into the *Abhidharma-mahāvibhāṣā* (c. 2nd century CE, two hundred fascicle), which became Sarvāstivādin orthodoxy; after that came Dharmasena's *Abhidharmahṛdaya* and Dharmatrāta's *Saṃyuktābhidharmahṛdaya*, until Vasubandhu's *Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya* (c. 4th–5th century CE) carried out a general review of the Sarvāstivāda from the standpoint of the Sautrāntika.

Chinese Buddhism therefore has a peculiar phenomenon: **“Abhidharma studies” (pi-tan) were once a prominent discipline in the Northern and Southern Dynasties**, and the *Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya* became the basis of the Kośa school — after the treatises of the Sthavira lineage had for a time been overshadowed by the Mahāyāna, they returned to the theoretical centre of the Chinese tradition in the form of “treatises.”

The framework of causal analysis established by the [[sarvastivada|Sarvāstivāda]], and the most detailed theory of causality in Buddhism.

**The six causes**: efficient cause, coexistent cause, homogeneous cause, associated cause, universally pervasive cause, and [[vipaka|ripening]] cause. **The four conditions**: causal condition, immediately antecedent condition, object condition, and dominant condition. **The five results**: ripening result, outflow result, disjunction result, virile-activity result, and dominant result.

This framework addresses a concrete problem: **dependent origination says that “when this exists, that comes to be,” but there is far more than one way of “existing.”** The relation of father and son, of fire and smoke, of one mental factor to the mind itself, of past wholesome karma to present pleasure — these differ in kind. If they are not distinguished, dependent origination degenerates into a catch-all formula; once they are distinguished, causality can be tested in concrete cases.

Several points among them:

- **Ripening cause and ripening result** — wholesome and unwholesome karma brings forth a morally neutral fruition (the cause is wholesome or unwholesome, the result is neither), hence “ripening” (vipāka);
- **Outflow cause and outflow result** — continuity within the same kind (a wholesome thought-moment giving rise to the next wholesome thought-moment);
- **The disjunction result** — not a result that is produced but “analytical cessation, the uncompounded (pratisaṃkhyā-nirodha),” that is, what is made manifest by the eradication of afflictions; it is therefore also called a “result” yet does not belong to the mutual production of cause and result;
- **The immediately antecedent condition among the four conditions** — laid down specifically for mental dharmas: when the preceding thought-moment ceases it vacates its place for the arising of the following thought-moment, and so mental dharmas continue in succession and cannot arise simultaneously.

Yogācāra adopts the four conditions while adjusting the six causes in order to explain the mutual perfuming of seeds and manifest activity. In reading the disputes between Madhyamaka and Yogācāra, this vocabulary is the shared background — neither side denies that causality has levels; the disagreement is whether a cause has own-nature.

One of the two great tracks of Indian Mahāyāna alongside [[madhyamaka|Madhyamaka]]; it is called the consciousness-only school because it holds that “[[vijnapti_matra|consciousness exists, the object does not]],” and the Yogācāra school because it follows the stages of practice and realisation of the “levels of the yogācāras.”

Lineage: **Maitreya** (the traditional author of the *Yogācārabhūmi-śāstra*, the *Madhyāntavibhāga*, and others, whose historicity is disputed — on one account Maitreya of the Tuṣita heaven, on another a historical person) → **Asaṅga** (c. 310–390, *Mahāyānasaṃgraha*, *Abhidharmasamuccaya*) → **Vasubandhu** (c. 320–400, *Triṃśikā*, *Viṃśatikā*, and also the *Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya*) → the ten great masters → Xuanzang and Kuiji.

The main trunk of the doctrine:

- **The eight consciousnesses**: the first five consciousnesses, the sixth, mind-consciousness, the seventh, manas, and the eighth, ālaya-consciousness;
- **The three natures**: the imagined, the dependent, and the perfected, as the structure of delusion and awakening;
- **Consciousness exists, the object does not**: the object is not apart from consciousness;
- **The five categories and one hundred dharmas**: all dharmas arranged into one hundred dharmas, the most complete classification of existents in Buddhism;
- **Transformation of consciousness into wisdom**: the eight consciousnesses are transformed and four wisdoms obtained, whereby one becomes a Buddha.

Its theoretical motive is **to preserve both non-self and causality**: if there is no real self and yet karma must continue across aeons, the most economical solution is to let “consciousness” carry the continuity without need of a substance. Yogācāra soteriology and ontology are therefore two faces of one structure.

The Chinese Faxiang school (Xuanzang–Kuiji), the Tibetan transmission, and the Japanese Yogācāra transmission read the question whether the dependent nature exists differently: the Chinese transmission broadly follows the position that “the dependent nature exists” (the Dharmapāla line), which stands in sharp opposition to the Prāsaṅgika Madhyamaka position that “the dependent nature too has no own-nature.”

The great mirror wisdom, the wisdom of equality, the wisdom of wondrous observation, and the wisdom of accomplishing what is to be done, produced by the transformation of the [[eight_consciousnesses|eight consciousnesses]].

- The great mirror wisdom (from the eighth consciousness): like a mirror reflecting all things without discrimination;
- The wisdom of equality (from the seventh consciousness): once self-grasping is cut off, it contemplates self and others as equal;
- The wisdom of wondrous observation (from the sixth consciousness): it skilfully observes the particular and the shared marks of all dharmas, and can turn the wheel of the Dharma and cut off the doubts of beings;
- The wisdom of accomplishing what is to be done (from the first five consciousnesses): it manifests various transformations in all worlds and accomplishes the work of benefiting others.

The four wisdoms are coordinated with the three bodies: the great mirror wisdom and the wisdom of equality correspond to the dharma-body and the enjoyment-body, while the wisdom of wondrous observation and the wisdom of accomplishing what is to be done correspond to the manifestation-body. This coordination gives the [[vijnapti_matra|consciousness-only]] theory of practice a definite structure of results, rather than stopping at “cutting off affliction”.

The *[[tathagatagarbha|Tathāgatagarbha]] Sūtra* (Da Fangdeng Rulai Zang Jing), the *[[doc_14|Śrīmālādevī Sūtra]]*, the *Anūnatvāpūrṇatvanirdeśa* (Bu Zeng Bu Jian Jing), the *Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra*, the *Laṅkāvatāra Sūtra*, the *Ratnagotravibhāga*, and others.

The common theme of this group of scriptures is: all sentient beings intrinsically possess the tathāgatagarbha and can therefore all attain Buddhahood; the mind-nature is intrinsically pure but is defiled by adventitious dust. Among them:
- The *Tathāgatagarbha Sūtra* is generally regarded as the earliest tathāgatagarbha scripture to appear; it uses nine similes to explain that the Tathāgata is present within the bodies of sentient beings;
- The *Śrīmālādevī Sūtra* proposes the distinction between “the empty tathāgatagarbha” and “the non-empty tathāgatagarbha”, and says that the tathāgatagarbha is “the store of the dharma-realm, the store of the dharma-body, the supramundane supreme store, and the store of intrinsic purity”;
- The *Ratnagotravibhāga* organises the meaning of the tathāgatagarbha by way of seven vajra points, and is the summary treatise of this tradition.

The formation of this group of scriptures falls roughly between the 3rd and 5th centuries CE, later than the Prajñāpāramitā sūtras (c. 1st–2nd century BCE) and earlier than the compilation of the Yogācāra treatises (4th–5th century); it therefore stands between the two main lines of “emptiness” and “consciousness” — it presupposes emptiness (the tathāgatagarbha is itself empty) while also providing a positive basis for attaining Buddhahood. It is precisely this position that led Chinese Buddhist thought (Tiantai, Huayan, Chan) to absorb it so extensively.

The two most controversial topics in the [[tathagatagarbha|tathāgatagarbha]] tradition, whose outcome determined the practical boundaries of Chinese Buddhism.

**The icchantika** (“one who desires”) refers to a person who has cut off all roots of good, does not believe in causality, and does not practise the pure life. **The problem**: the first half of the *[[doc_7|Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra]]* maintains that “the icchantika cannot attain Buddhahood”, while the second half says that “all sentient beings possess the Buddha-nature”, so that the icchantika too has the Buddha-nature and will also attain Buddhahood — the discrepancy between the earlier and later parts of one and the same scripture is precisely the site of this controversy.

- **Yogācāra (the doctrine of the five lineages)** — it posits “the lineage-less”, who can never attain Buddhahood; this doctrine corresponds to “the icchantika does not attain Buddhahood”;
- **Chinese Buddhism (the later part of the *Nirvāṇa Sūtra* and the *Lotus Sūtra*)** — it adopts “all sentient beings possess the Buddha-nature” and judges that the icchantika too will attain Buddhahood, which became the settled conclusion;
- **Zhu Daosheng** — before the later part of the *Nirvāṇa Sūtra* had been translated (c. early 5th century) he already maintained that “all icchantikas can attain Buddhahood”, was judged by the monks of his time to be teaching heresy and was expelled, and after the scriptures were translated his thesis was confirmed, an event known in history as “first to illumine alone” (gu ming xian fa). This is **the most famous case in Chinese Buddhist history of a single doctrine maintained against the many and finally vindicated**.

**Permanence, bliss, self, and purity** (the four virtues) are the reversal of the four inversions of “suffering, impermanence, non-self, and impurity”: the *Nirvāṇa Sūtra* says that the Tathāgata's dharma-body possesses the four virtues of permanence, bliss, self, and purity. This is a key reminder for readers of the scriptures — **one must not take “non-self and impermanence” as the whole conclusion of Buddhism**: on the level of “all sentient beings possess the Buddha-nature”, the tathāgatagarbha tradition uses an affirmative vocabulary (true permanence, wondrous existence), running parallel to the via negativa vocabulary of the Āgamas and Madhyamaka. How these two vocabularies are to be reconciled is precisely the core problem addressed by Chinese doctrinal classification.

“Panjiao,” or doctrinal classification, is the graded evaluation of the scriptures and teachings — arranging the many sūtras and treatises in sequence according to the time of their preaching, the capacities of their audiences, and the depth of their doctrine, so that scriptures that appear mutually contradictory each receive their proper place. Doctrinal classification is the method by which each school establishes its own system, and it is also the necessary intermediary in the sequence “[[tathagatagarbha|tathāgatagarbha]] → the schools”: the schools are not derived directly from tathāgatagarbha, but first locate themselves through doctrinal classification and then organise their own core doctrines according to the position so assigned.

The principal Chinese systems of doctrinal classification:
- Tiantai's “[[wushi_bajiao|five periods and eight teachings]]” — the five periods (Avataṃsaka, Āgama, Vaipulya, Prajñāpāramitā, Lotus and Nirvāṇa), the eight teachings being divided into the “four teachings in respect of method” (sudden, gradual, secret, indeterminate) and the “four teachings of doctrine” (Tripiṭaka, common, distinct, perfect), taking the *Lotus Sūtra* as purely perfect and uniquely sublime;
- Huayan's “five teachings and ten schools” — the five teachings of the Hīnayāna, the initial, the final, the sudden, and the perfect, taking the *Avataṃsaka Sūtra* as the one vehicle of the distinct teaching;
- Yogācāra (Ci'en) “three periods” — the teaching of existence, the teaching of emptiness, and the teaching of the middle way of definitive meaning, taking the *Saṃdhinirmocana-sūtra* as the third period;
- Sanlun's “two collections and three turnings” — the collection of the hearers and the collection of the bodhisattvas; the root dharma-wheel, the branch dharma-wheel, and the dharma-wheel of gathering the branches back to the root.

The significance of doctrinal classification is twofold: on one side it is theoretical integration — it gives the diversity of the scriptures an organising principle; on the other it is sectarian position — every system judges the scripture on which its own school relies to be the most ultimate. Doctrinal classification is therefore both the mode by which Indian Buddhist learning was reorganised in China and the mark of the establishment of the Chinese schools.

Identity in principle, identity in name, identity in contemplative practice, identity in resemblance, identity in partial realisation, and ultimate identity. They mark the six stages from ordinary person to Buddha, neither exalting the sage's realm too high nor belittling oneself.

Zhiyi established this doctrine in order to “counteract two illnesses.” *[[doc_39|Mohe Zhiguan]]*, fascicle 1: “These six identities begin with the ordinary and end with the sage; because they begin with the ordinary, doubt and timidity are removed; because they end with the sage, arrogant conceit is removed.” “Six” indicates the depth and shallowness of the grades, removing the illness of resigning oneself to decline; “identity” indicates that the very substance just is [Buddhahood], removing the illness of overweening pride.

The content of the six identities (according to the *Mohe Zhiguan* and the *[[doc_34|Fahua Xuanyi]]*):
- Identity in principle: all sentient beings fully possess the principle of the three thousand and the three truths without any deficiency; a single moment of mind is the principle of tathāgatagarbha;
- Identity in name: having heard the teaching, one understands the principle of the three thousand and the three truths and has no doubt regarding the Buddhadharma of the ten directions and three times;
- Identity in contemplative practice: moment after moment one contemplates the principle of the three thousand and the three truths without interruption (the outer ordinary [stage]; the position of the five grades of disciples);
- Identity in resemblance: contemplation continues until the two afflictions of view and thought are exhausted, and one attains the purity of the six sense faculties (the inner ordinary [stage]; the position of the ten faiths);
- Identity in partial truth (partial realisation): ignorance is gradually removed and the principle-substance of the dharma-nature is partially manifested (from the ten abidings to the position of equal enlightenment);
- Ultimate identity: the affliction of ignorance is exhausted and the principle-substance of the dharma-nature is ultimately manifested (the position of wondrous enlightenment).

Master Yinguang's explanation is vivid: “Take a newborn child: in bodily form it is no different from its parents, yet in power and function the two are vastly far apart. One cannot say that the newborn child is not a person, nor can one make the child undertake the affairs of an adult.”

The dharma-realm of phenomena, the dharma-realm of principle, the dharma-realm of the non-obstruction of principle and phenomena, and the dharma-realm of the non-obstruction of phenomenon with phenomenon.

- The dharma-realm of phenomena: all the dharmas of form and mind throughout the universe each have their own characteristics, each with its own bounds, without confusing one another;
- The dharma-realm of principle: although dharmas differ in a thousand ways, none has substantial reality, and the true reality of [[suchness|suchness]] is ultimately equal;
- The dharma-realm of the non-obstruction of principle and phenomena: the differentiated marks of phenomena and the equal nature of principle interfuse without obstruction — principle is revealed through phenomena, and phenomena are constituted by embracing principle;
- The dharma-realm of the non-obstruction of phenomenon with phenomenon: all dharmas of form and mind are dependently arisen and without substantial reality; the great can contain the small and the small can contain the great, layer upon layer without end.

Its process of formation deserves attention: Dushun's *Huayan Fajie Guan Men* established three contemplations (the contemplation of true emptiness, the contemplation of the non-obstruction of principle and phenomena, and the contemplation of universal containment); Zhiyan and Fazang developed the [[ten_gates|ten mysterious gates]]; and Chengguan, in the *Huayan Fajie Xuanjing*, replaced the three contemplations with “dharma-realm” (the contemplation of true emptiness → the dharma-realm of principle; the contemplation of the non-obstruction of principle and phenomena → the dharma-realm of the non-obstruction of principle and phenomena; the contemplation of universal containment → the dharma-realm of the non-obstruction of phenomenon with phenomenon), and added “the dharma-realm of phenomena” as the basis on which the three contemplations rest. With this the names and the system of the four dharma-realms were complete.

Chengguan further correlated the four dharma-realms with doctrinal classification: the Hīnayāna teaching belongs to the dharma-realm of phenomena; the Mahāyāna initial teaching is divided between phenomena and principle; the final teaching belongs to the non-obstruction of principle and phenomena; the sudden teaching belongs to the dharma-realm of principle; and the perfect teaching belongs to the non-obstruction of phenomenon with phenomenon.

They explain the relations of mutual identity and mutual interpenetration in the non-obstruction of phenomenon with phenomenon from ten angles. They are mutually explanatory with the [[six_aspects|six characteristics in mutual interfusion]], and the two have traditionally been called together “the ten mysterious gates and the six characteristics,” jointly forming the central content of [[dharma_realm_origination|dharmadhātu dependent origination]].

There is a distinction between the “old ten mysterious gates” and the “new ten mysterious gates”: those established by Zhiyan in the *Huayan Yisheng Shixuan Men* and inherited by Fazang in the *Huayan Yisheng Jiaoyi Fenqi Zhang* (the *Five Teachings Chapter*) are the old ten mysterious gates; those set out by Fazang in fascicle 1 of the *Huayan Jing Tanxuan Ji* and followed by Chengguan in fascicle 6 of the *Huayan Xuantan* are the new ten mysterious gates.

The gates of the new ten mysterious gates (the first gate is the general statement, the remaining nine are particular statements):
1. The gate of simultaneous complete correspondence — the dependent-origination interconnection by which all things, pervading time and space, become one body, perfectly complete, without any distinction of before and after, beginning and end (the general statement);
2. The gate of the free non-obstruction of the broad and the narrow — the great has nothing outside it, the small has nothing it does not include, and the small can contain the great (simile: a mirror one foot wide can show the reflections of a thousand miles);
3. The gate of the mutual containment of one and many without their becoming the same — one and many interpenetrate without losing the marks of one and many (simile: a thousand lamps in one room, each light interacting with the others);
4. The gate of the free mutual identity of all dharmas — spoken from the side of substance, emptiness and existence are mutually identical (simile: gold and the colour of gold are inseparable);
5. The gate of the secret and the manifest both accomplished — one dharma is all dharmas, and whether hidden or manifest, hidden and manifest are both accomplished;
6. The gate of the establishment of minute mutual containment — because nature is empty, the great can contain the small and the small can contain the great; within a single hair and a single mote of dust there are phenomenal marks;
7. The gate of the realm of Indra's net — within each and every dharma there are immeasurable dharmas, layer upon layer reflecting one another without exhaustion;
8. The gate of relying on phenomena to reveal the dharma and give rise to understanding — revealing principle through phenomena;
9. The gate of the ten times whose distinct dharmas are differently accomplished — each of the three times possesses the three times, making nine times, and the nine times interpenetrate to form together one total time (“to know that immeasurable kalpas are a single moment, to know that a single moment is immeasurable kalpas”);
10. The gate of host and companion perfectly bright and endowed with virtues — whenever any one dharma is taken as host, the remaining dharmas are companions, host and companions mutually illuminating.

The dharma-body, the enjoyment-body, and the transformation-body.

- The dharma-body (dharmakāya): the true reality of all dharmas, the principle-substance of [[suchness|suchness]], without marks and pervading all things;
- The enjoyment-body (saṃbhogakāya): the perfect body of fruition brought about by cultivation on the causal ground, such as Amitābha Buddha and Rocana Buddha (Luzhena);
- The transformation-body (nirmāṇakāya): the body manifested in response to sentient beings' capacities, such as Śākyamuni Buddha.

The doctrine of the three bodies has two uses: first, “the three bodies as one substance” — the three bodies are three faces of the same Buddha, spoken from the three sides of principle, wisdom, and function; second, “the three bodies as distinct” — the dharma-body is the substance, and the enjoyment-body and transformation-body are the function. The schools differ in emphasis: Huayan takes Vairocana as the dharma-body Buddha; [[vijnapti_matra|consciousness-only]] holds that the three bodies are attained through the transformation of consciousness into wisdom (the four wisdoms correlated with the three bodies).

The significance of the doctrine of the three bodies lies in handling the complexity of “the Buddha”: it says both that the Buddha transcends speech and thought (the dharma-body) and that the Buddha has a historical manifestation (the transformation-body), while also explaining the perfection of the fruit of practice (the enjoyment-body). This structure enables Buddhist Buddhology to accommodate at once the philosophically absolute and the religiously approachable.

The pure land brought about by the power of Amitābha Buddha's vows, where those reborn do not fall back and attain Buddhahood. “Sukhāvatī” means “possessing bliss”; the sūtras describe the sentient beings of that land as having none of the many sufferings but experiencing only various pleasures, and the core of “having none of the many sufferings” lies in “non-retrogression” — those reborn no longer fall back.

The nature of Sukhāvatī is understood differently among the schools:
- The [[pureland|Pure Land]] school (Shandao) judges it a “reward land,” which ordinary beings can enter by [[two_paths_two_powers|other-power]];
- Yogācāra and some Tiantai commentators judge it a “transformation land,” manifested in response to capacities;
- Tiantai has in addition the doctrine of the “four lands” (the land of co-residence of ordinary beings and sages, the land of expedient remainder, the land of true reward without obstruction, and the land of eternal quiescent light), and judges Sukhāvatī to be, for instance, the land of co-residence of ordinary beings and sages.

These differences do not affect the practical core of the Pure Land school (faith, vows, and recitation of the name), but they do affect the explanation of the nature of “rebirth” — whether one is born into a real land adorned by true reward or enters a provisional land of responsive transformation.

The mystery of the body, the mystery of speech and the mystery of mind, in correspondence with the Buddha's three mysteries.

- The mystery of the body: bodily acts such as forming mudras and sitting cross-legged in the lotus posture;
- The mystery of speech: reciting mantras (dharani);
- The mystery of mind: mental acts such as visualising the deity and the moon-disc.

'Correspondence' (yoga) is the keyword: the three are not three independent exercises but a single state in which the three modes of action interpenetrate the Buddha's three modes of action. Esoteric practice therefore does not turn on 'cutting off' but on 'transmuting': afflictions and karma are regarded as transposable into the Buddha's qualities rather than as things that must be destroyed. This stance of 'transmuting affliction into awakening' differs markedly in method from the exoteric 'cutting off of affliction', though the goal is the same.

The [[tiantai|Tiantai school]]'s [[panjiao|doctrinal classification]] system established by Zhiyi, which arranges all the scriptures in a graded order. The substance of 'doctrinal classification' is **to provide scriptural warrant for a school's position**: to judge one scripture 'perfect' and another 'provisional' is also to establish the definitive status of the scripture on which one's own school relies.

**The five periods** (according to the chronological order of the Buddha's preaching)

1. **The Avataṃsaka period** - immediately after his awakening the Buddha taught the *Avataṃsaka Sutra*, directly revealing the realm of the Buddha's fruit, but those of small capacity were as if deaf and dumb;
2. **The Deer Park period** (the Agama period) - for those of shallow capacity he taught the four Agamas, setting out the four truths and dependent origination;
3. **The Vaipulya period** - he taught the Vaipulya scriptures, striking at one-sidedness, censuring the small, praising the great and commending the perfect;
4. **The Prajñā period** - he taught the Prajñā scriptures, breaking every attachment and revealing emptiness;
5. **The Lotus and Nirvāṇa period** - he taught the *Lotus Sutra*, opening the provisional and revealing the real, gathering the three into one; and finally taught the *Nirvāṇa Sutra*, upholding the precepts and expounding permanence.

**The eight teachings** fall into two groups: the **four teachings in respect of method** (the form of the teaching) - sudden, gradual, secret and indeterminate; and the **four teachings in respect of doctrine** (the content of the teaching) - Tripitaka, common, distinct and perfect (the Tripitaka teaching refers to the Hīnayāna tripitaka, the common teaching is shared by great and small, the distinct teaching belongs to bodhisattvas alone, and the perfect teaching is complete).

**The judgement that the *Lotus Sutra* is 'purely perfect and uniquely sublime'**: the scriptures before the *Lotus* are 'provisional teachings', while the *Lotus* reveals 'opening the Buddha's knowledge and vision', and is therefore the most definitive. This judgement directly conflicts with the Faxiang school's 'three-period classification' (which holds the *Saṃdhinirmocana-sūtra* to be the definitive teaching of the third period): the same body of scriptures arranged in two ways, behind which stands the opposition over whether Buddhahood has limits (the doctrine of the five lineages versus 'all sentient beings possess the Buddha-nature').

The [[huayan|Huayan school]]'s [[panjiao|doctrinal classification]] system of Fazang, standing alongside Tiantai's 'five periods and eight teachings' but proceeding differently: Tiantai arranges the scriptures by time, whereas Huayan arranges the doctrines by **the depth of the teaching**.

**The five teachings**

1. **The Hīnayāna teaching** - sets out the four truths and the twelve links, explaining the emptiness of the person;
2. **The initial teaching of the Great Vehicle** - that is, the first stage of Yogacara and Madhyamaka, teaching that all is empty or that consciousness exists while its objects do not;
3. **The final teaching of the Great Vehicle** - teaching suchness-based dependent origination and that all sentient beings possess the Buddha-nature;
4. **The sudden teaching of the Great Vehicle** - beyond words and conceptual thought, pointing directly at the true reality (close to the Chan approach);
5. **The perfect teaching of the one vehicle** - namely the *Avataṃsaka*, teaching the non-obstruction of phenomenon with phenomenon and that the one is the all.

**The ten schools** are a further division into ten categories according to doctrinal content, used to assign the doctrinal levels of the various schools of the time, culminating in the 'school of perfect illumination and complete virtue' (Huayan).

A comparison with Tiantai's divisions brings out the difference in character between the two schools:

- Tiantai's 'five periods' is a classification by **time and pedagogical method**, stressing 'why the Buddha spoke thus' (pedagogy);
- Huayan's 'five teachings' is a classification by **doctrinal level**, stressing 'the relative height of the doctrines' (doctrinal theology).

**The judgement that the *Avataṃsaka* is the 'one vehicle of the distinct teaching'**: the *Lotus* is the 'one vehicle of the common teaching' (gathering the three into one, the three vehicles entering the one together), whereas the *Avataṃsaka* is the 'one vehicle of the distinct teaching' (not shared with the three vehicles, the realm of Buddhas alone). By this Fazang establishes the *Avataṃsaka*'s supreme place among all scriptures.

The core of [[vinaya|Vinaya school]] theory, deriving from Daoxuan's (596-667) interpretation of the *[[doc_25|Dharmaguptaka Vinaya]]*: all the precepts can be explained under four categories - **the dharma of the precepts** (the standard of the rules), **the essence of the precepts** (what is obtained at ordination, the capacity to prevent wrong and stop evil), **the practice of the precepts** (the practice of upholding them on the basis of that essence), and **the characteristics of the precepts** (the differentiated features of the rules).

Among these, 'the essence of the precepts' is the most theoretical element in Vinaya school thought and the focus of dispute among the sectarian schools: what exactly is obtained at ordination, such that a subsequent 'not committing evil' has an inner basis?

- **The Sarvastivadins** - posit 'non-manifest matter' (avijnapti-rupa): the essence of the precepts is an invisible but real material dharma that persists as a result of the enacting of bodily and verbal karma;
- **The Sautrantikas** - deny the reality of non-manifest matter and hold that the essence of the precepts is the function of a 'seed of intention' (cetana-bija) on the mental continuum;
- **The Satyasiddhi-sastra** - posits an essence of the precepts that is 'neither matter nor mind', mediating between the two views;
- **Daoxuan (Nanshan Vinaya)** - following the *Satyasiddhi-sastra*, posits a seed-essence of the precepts that is 'neither matter nor mind', and judges the *Dharmaguptaka Vinaya* to be 'permeated by the Great Vehicle', taking the 'essence of the precepts of the perfect teaching' as definitive.

**Why this matters**: the question of the essence of the precepts turns 'the precepts' from a code of behaviour into **an ontological and psychological question**: the precepts are not merely 'not doing certain things' but a continuing capacity in the person ordained. This treatment allowed the Vinaya school to connect with Yogacara and tathāgatagarbha theories of seeds and of mind-nature, and became a theoretical hallmark of Chinese precept studies.

The four classes into which the tantras are divided according to their mode of practice and their aim:

- **Kriya-tantra** - chiefly external purification, offering and recitation of mantras, with the deity as a guest;
- **Carya-tantra** - external acts and [[southern_tradition|inner contemplation]] in equal measure;
- **Yoga-tantra** - stresses inner samadhi and visualisation, with the deity as a friend;
- **Anuttarayoga-tantra** - the highest level, in which the deity is oneself ([[sokushin_jobutsu|attaining Buddhahood in this very body]]); in Tibetan Buddhism it is further divided into father tantras, mother tantras and non-dual tantras.

The point of the classification is to determine **the stages of practice and the precepts**: each of the four classes has its own initiations, samaya precepts, mandalas and rituals, and practice in a higher class must rest on the ethical basis of the lower. The 'four classes' are therefore not merely a ranking by difficulty but a complete system of training.

Their transmission to China and Tibet differed greatly: **the Chinese transmission (Tang esoteric Buddhism)** relies chiefly on the *Mahavairocana-abhisambodhi-tantra* (garbhadhatu) and the *Vajrasekhara-tantra* (vajradhatu) and belongs largely to the yoga class and below; **the Tibetan transmission** possesses all four classes and gives weight to anuttarayoga - a difference that makes the doctrinal emphases and actual practices of Chinese and Tibetan esoteric Buddhism quite distinct.

The school configuration formed in Tibetan Buddhism after the earlier diffusion (c. 7th-9th centuries) and the later diffusion (from the 11th century):

- **Nyingma** (the ancient school) - heir to the old-translation tantras of the Padmasambhava line of the earlier diffusion, with Dzogchen (the Great Perfection) as its highest teaching;
- **Kagyu** - stresses oral transmission and practical cultivation (the six dharmas of Naropa, mahamudra), the line of Marpa and Milarepa;
- **Sakya** - takes the path-and-fruit (lamdre) teaching as its doctrine, and was the mainstream of Tibetan religion and politics under the Yuan (Phagpa was Kublai Khan's imperial preceptor);
- **Jonang** - holds the 'empty-of-other' view (that the [[tathagatagarbha|tathāgatagarbha]] is truly existent and that adventitious stains are what is empty of other), standing apart within Tibetan Buddhism and later suppressed by the Gelug;
- **Gelug** - formed by Tsongkhapa's reform, taking Prasangika [[madhyamaka|Madhyamaka]] as the definitive view and the *Lamrim Chenmo* as the general outline of study, with the Dalai and Panchen lines as its reincarnation successions.

**A contrast with the Chinese transmission**: in Tibetan Buddhism the weight of doctrinal classification lies on the 'stages of sutra and tantra' and on 'Madhyamaka insight', whereas in the Chinese transmission it lies on 'scriptures and schools'; Tibetan monastic education centres on debate in pramana (Buddhist logic), Chinese on doctrine-and-contemplation and practical methods. Both inherit Indian Buddhism, but their approaches and degree of institutionalisation differ greatly.

**Two misunderstandings common among readers in China**: the first is to equate Tibetan Buddhism with 'esoteric Buddhism' - in fact its exoteric doctrine (Madhyamaka, pramana, precepts) is extremely complete, and tantric practice is only the upper part of its system of study; the second is to regard the 'reincarnation of living Buddhas' as unique to Tibetan Buddhism - the reincarnation system was in fact the product of gradual institutionalisation by the Kagyu, Gelug and other schools from the 13th century onwards in order to secure their successions.

In the [[sanlun|Sanlun school]], Jizang's layer-by-layer refutation of the [[two_truths|two truths]] is the concrete operation of the method that 'establishes no position of its own'. The four levels are as follows:

1. 'Existence' is taken as conventional truth and 'emptiness' as ultimate truth;
2. 'Existence and emptiness' is taken as conventional truth and 'neither existence nor emptiness' as ultimate truth;
3. 'The non-duality of the two (the duality of existence and emptiness)' is taken as conventional truth and 'neither the two nor the non-dual' as ultimate truth;
4. The first three levels are all taken as conventional truth and 'non-attainment' as ultimate truth.

**The purpose of the four levels**: each level turns the 'ultimate' of the previous level into the 'conventional' of the next. Ultimate truth is not a conclusion reached once and for all but a step transcended layer by layer. By the fourth level 'ultimate truth' itself has become an expedient to be let go, and the point of rest is 'non-attainment'.

This procedure is continuous with the apophatic method of the *Mulamadhyamakakarika*, but in China it was systematised into operable levels of interpretation. **It also explains why the Sanlun school does not engage in doctrinal classification**: classification assigns ranks to the scriptures, whereas the fourfold twofold truth is meant to cancel all ranking; the two are mutually exclusive in method.

Contemplation of the body, contemplation of feeling, contemplation of mind, contemplation of dharmas. These are the specific content of [[right_mindfulness|right mindfulness]] and the most direct point of entry on the path to liberation.

The four establishments counter the four inversions — "permanence, pleasure, self, purity" — by way of "the body is impure, feeling is suffering, mind is impermanent, [[nonself_seal|dharmas are without self]]." Their method of practice is unfolded in extreme detail in the *Mahāsatipaṭṭhāna Sutta*:
- contemplation of the body: mindfulness of breathing, the postures (walking, standing, sitting, lying down), clear comprehension, the impurities (the thirty-two parts of the body), analysis into the four elements, and the cemetery contemplations;
- contemplation of feeling: observing the three feelings of painful, pleasant, and neither-painful-nor-pleasant, and whether they are defiled or undefiled;
- contemplation of mind: observing the eight pairs and sixteen kinds of mental phenomena (with lust / without lust, with hatred / without hatred, with delusion / without delusion, contracted / distracted, exalted / unexalted, surpassable / unsurpassable, concentrated / unconcentrated, liberated / unliberated);
- contemplation of dharmas: observing in accordance with the Dharma the five hindrances, the five aggregates of clinging, the six internal and external bases, the seven factors of enlightenment, and the four noble truths.

The *Mahāsatipaṭṭhāna Sutta* calls this the "one way" (ekāyano maggo): "It is for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow and lamentation, for the destruction of pain and grief, for reaching the right path, for the realisation of nirvāṇa." The four establishments of mindfulness stand first among the thirty-seven aids to enlightenment.

Note: the standing of the four establishments differs between the Southern and the Chinese traditions — the Southern tradition (especially the modern insight movement), taking the Theravāda view, regards them as the method by which the Buddha himself attained awakening and accords them the highest importance; Mahāsī Sayādaw produced a Pali–Burmese bilingual edition of the *Mahāsatipaṭṭhāna Sutta*. Although the Chinese Mahāyāna tradition likewise has the advocacy of the *Mahāprajñāpāramitā-śāstra*, fascicle 48, and the elaboration in four fascicle of Zhiyi's *Si Nianchu* (*The Four Establishments of Mindfulness*), its overall emphasis is lower than that of the Southern tradition.

Loving-kindness, compassion, sympathetic joy, and equanimity. They are an important method for cultivating concentration and the basis of the Mahāyāna aspiration to awakening. They are also called the "four divine abidings" (brahmavihāra), because their fruit can be rebirth in the Brahma heavens.

- Loving-kindness (mettā / maitrī): the wish that beings obtain happiness;
- Compassion (karuṇā): the wish that beings be free from suffering;
- Sympathetic joy (muditā): rejoicing when beings obtain happiness, counteracting envy;
- Equanimity (upekṣā): being equal and without discrimination towards beings, free from liking and aversion.

The sense of "immeasurable" (apramāṇa) lies in the immeasurability of the object: the attitude is not directed at one particular being but extends to every direction and every being, and so the measure of the mind is unbounded. The order of the four is not accidental: loving-kindness and compassion have a definite orientation (giving happiness, removing suffering); sympathetic joy further counteracts displeasure at others' happiness; and equanimity prevents the first three from falling into partiality — without equanimity, loving-kindness may become favouritism and compassion may become indulgence.

Their place in the cultivation of concentration: the four immeasurable attitudes can be developed up to the level of the "third dhyāna" (sympathetic joy) and the "fourth dhyāna" (equanimity), and are one of the specialised objects of calm (śamatha) practice; in the Mahāyāna they are the preliminary to the aspiration to awakening — one first softens the character with the four immeasurable attitudes, and then generates the aspiration, "for the benefit of all beings, may I attain Buddhahood."

Giving, kindly speech, beneficial conduct, and cooperation. These are the four methods by which the bodhisattva gathers sentient beings.

- Giving: first relieving their want with material and Dharma gifts, so that they come to trust one;
- Kindly speech: greeting and encouraging them with pleasant words, truthful words, and words in accordance with the Dharma, so that they give rise to faith and understanding;
- Beneficial conduct: performing actions that benefit others, so that they take up practice in accordance with the teaching;
- Cooperation: dwelling and acting together with the beings to be converted, sharing their undertakings, so that they have no doubt or fear.

The sequence of the four implies a pedagogy: first relieve their distress (giving), then communicate with their minds (kindly speech), then guide their conduct (beneficial conduct), and finally go along with them (cooperation) — from establishing a relationship to practising together. The four means of gathering are therefore not a list of four good deeds but a methodology of "living with people and guiding them."

Their difference from [[four_immeasurables|the four immeasurable attitudes]]: the four immeasurable attitudes are "the direction of the mind" (the level of aspiration), while the four means of gathering are "the manner of action" (the operational level). In combination the two constitute the complete structure of Mahāyāna altruistic practice.

The four dhyānas of the [[three_realms|form realm]] and the four concentrations of the formless realm, together called the four dhyānas and eight concentrations. These are the levels of concentration described under [[right_samadhi|right concentration]].

The four dhyānas of the form realm:
- First dhyāna: secluded from sense-desires and unwholesome dharmas, with applied and sustained attention, with joy and happiness born of seclusion;
- Second dhyāna: applied and sustained attention stilled, inner clarity, with joy and happiness born of concentration;
- Third dhyāna: free of joy, dwelling in equanimity, with mindfulness and clear comprehension, experiencing happiness with the body ("the marvellous happiness of freedom from joy");
- Fourth dhyāna: equanimity and purity of mindfulness, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling.

The four concentrations of the formless realm: the sphere of infinite space, the sphere of infinite consciousness, the sphere of nothingness, and the sphere of neither-perception-nor-non-perception.

The śāstras describe the four dhyānas with the "factors" (dhyāna-aṅga) as their framework: the first dhyāna has five factors (applied attention, sustained attention, joy, happiness, one-pointedness of mind); the second has four (inner clarity, joy, happiness, one-pointedness of mind); the third has five (equanimity, mindfulness, clear comprehension, happiness, one-pointedness of mind); the fourth has four (equanimity, purity of mindfulness, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling, one-pointedness of mind).

Two points call for attention: first, the four dhyānas and eight concentrations are not unique to Buddhism, and non-Buddhists can attain them too, so that they are a "common practice"; second, the fourth dhyāna is the support for Buddhist insight and for the development of supernormal powers — the Buddha's awakening and his entry into nirvāṇa both occurred in the fourth dhyāna, because in that dhyāna equanimity and mindfulness are purified and the mind is most capable.

The thirty-seven items of practice leading to awakening, the most complete unfolding of the "[[marga|truth of the path]]" in early Buddhism. They fall into seven groups:

1. **[[four_foundations|The four establishments of mindfulness]]** — contemplation of body, feeling, mind, and dharmas;
2. **The four right efforts** — to cause arisen evil to be cut off, to prevent unarisen evil from arising, to cause unarisen good to arise, to cause arisen good to increase;
3. **The four bases of success** — desire, effort, mind, and investigation (the four bases for the accomplishment of concentration);
4. **The five faculties** — faith, effort, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom;
5. **The five powers** — the same five grown to the point of being unmoved by defilements;
6. **The seven factors of enlightenment** — mindfulness, investigation of dharmas, effort, joy, tranquillity, concentration, and equanimity (the sequence of enlightenment factors at the time of seeing the path);
7. **The noble eightfold path** — from right view to right concentration.

These seven groups are not seven parallel methods but **different cross-sections of one and the same process of practice**: the four establishments of mindfulness are the point of entry, the four right efforts the incitement, the four bases of success the power of concentration, the five faculties and five powers the growth and firming of mental strength, the seven factors of enlightenment the sequence of awakening-awareness as one approaches the seeing of the path, and the noble eightfold path the general outline of the whole. The śāstras therefore regard them as the inventory of the content of the "truth of the path."

Among them the position of the "seven factors of enlightenment" is the most telling: it describes the **sequence of mental activity before and after the seeing of the path** (mindfulness → investigation of dharmas → effort → joy → tranquillity → concentration → equanimity), the turning point of practice from exertion to letting go, and is therefore often singled out as a checklist for meditation.

The four fruitions of the hearer's practice, each having a "path" (the stage of practice tending towards that fruit) and a "fruit" (the stage already realised), together forming the "four paths and four fruits":

- **Sotāpanna** (stream-entry) — cuts off the three fetters (personality view, clinging to rules and vows, doubt), and at most returns seven times among gods and humans before attaining liberation;
- **Sakadāgāmin** (once-returner) — the three fetters are cut off and greed, hatred, and delusion are attenuated; returns once more to the [[three_realms|the realm of desire]];
- **Anāgāmin** (non-returner) — cuts off the five lower fetters, is no longer reborn in the realm of desire, and attains [[nirvana|nirvāṇa]] in the form realm;
- **Arhat** (worthy of offerings, without birth) — cuts off the five higher fetters (greed for the form realm, greed for the formless realm, conceit, restlessness, ignorance): "birth is exhausted, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more coming to any state of being."

**Two structural points deserve attention**

1. **The dividing line between the path of seeing and the path of cultivation** — the first fruit is the "path of seeing" (seeing the truth of the four noble truths and cutting off the defilements of view at once), and the last three fruits are the "path of cultivation" (gradually cutting off the defilements of cultivation). This division was retained by all the schools and by the later Mahāyāna theories of the stages: **the question of the relation between sudden awakening and gradual cultivation was first raised within the framework of the four fruits**.
2. **The sequence of fetters cut off has content** — it is not a set of abstract stage names but a concrete inventory of the defilements cut off (the three fetters, the five lower fetters, the five higher fetters). The four fruits are therefore "verifiable milestones of practice," not purely a hierarchy of belief.

The Mahāyāna assessed them in two ways: the Yogācāra (consciousness-only) school took the four fruits as the ultimate stage of the "Hīnayāna" and held that such persons really exist; the Tiantai and Huayan schools took them as a conjured city within the Mahāyāna path (the "conjured city" parable of the *Lotus Sūtra*), a resting place established as an expedient means.

The five stages of Mahāyāna practice, set out most completely in the [[vijnapti_matra|Yogācāra (consciousness-only)]] teaching ([[doc_29|Cheng Weishi Lun]], fascicle 9), though it has become a framework common to all schools:

1. **The stage of provisions** — generating the aspiration to awakening and accumulating the provisions of merit and wisdom (the ten faiths, ten abidings, ten practices, ten dedications);
2. **The stage of preparation** — cultivating the four preparations (heat, summit, acceptance, supreme worldly dharma) and approaching the path of seeing through śamatha and vipaśyanā;
3. **The stage of penetration** (the path of seeing) — cutting off at once the defilements and the obstructions to knowledge that arise from discrimination, and directly realising the suchness of the two emptinesses;
4. **The stage of cultivation** (the path of cultivation) — gradually cutting off the two obstructions that arise concomitantly, traversing the ten bhūmis through three great immeasurable aeons;
5. **The ultimate stage** — the two obstructions utterly cut off and the two emptinesses perfected, Buddhahood attained, and consciousness transformed into wisdom so that the four wisdoms are obtained.

**The key distinction in this framework is that between "arising from discrimination" and "arising concomitantly"**: what the path of seeing cuts off is the "defilements arising from discrimination" (views produced by mistaken learning after birth), while what the path of cultivation cuts off is the "defilements arising concomitantly" (instinctual clinging that comes into being together with life). There is therefore an intermediate state of a practitioner who has "seen the path but not yet become a Buddha" — one who has understood the principle but whose habitual tendencies are not yet exhausted, and this is precisely the theoretical point at issue in the dispute over "sudden awakening and gradual cultivation."

Their relation to the four fruits of the hearers: the path of seeing is the stage of the first fruit (stream-entry), while the utmost of the Mahāyāna is the ultimate stage (Buddhahood). The same framework can therefore accommodate the three vehicles, differing only in the end point and in the obstructions cut off (the Mahāyāna additionally cuts off the "obstruction to knowledge").

The Buddha's three bodies and the lands in which they dwell — the product of the Mahāyāna's expansion of "the Buddha" from the historical Śākyamuni into a cosmological concept.

- **The dharma body** (dharmakāya) — the [[suchness|suchness]] and dharma-nature itself that the Buddha realised, pervading all places, neither material nor mental, the ultimate sense of "Buddha";
- **The enjoyment body** (saṃbhogakāya) — the adorned body of fruition produced by aeons of practice, visible only to bodhisattvas, such as Amitābha Buddha and Vairocana Buddha;
- **The transformation body** (nirmāṇakāya) — the body manifested in response in order to save beings, such as Śākyamuni Buddha, which is subject to birth and death.

The Buddha-land (buddhakṣetra) corresponds in three ways: the land of dharma-nature (the dwelling of the dharma body, namely the principle of suchness), the land of true reward (the dwelling of the enjoyment body), and the land of response and transformation (the land manifested by the transformation body, such as [[kalpa_cosmology|the Sahā world]] and the Land of Bliss).

**The function of this set of concepts** It disposes of the dilemma of "becoming a Buddha": if the Buddha were only the historical physical body, how could he "save beings" after entering nirvāṇa? If the Buddha were said to exist eternally, that would contradict impermanence. The doctrine of the three bodies gives a third way — **omnipresent in dharma-nature, manifest in the power of vows, and responding in accordance with conditions**. It is therefore a framework accepted in common by the three systems of Madhyamaka (the dharma body as emptiness), Yogācāra (the enjoyment body as transformed by consciousness), and tathāgatagarbha (the dharma body as the tathāgatagarbha freed from adventitious defilement), differing only in names and emphases.

The actual operation of [[precepts|the precepts]] within the monastic community — the third level of the "[[sila|training in ethics]]": the precepts themselves (their marks), the essence of the precepts (obtained at ordination), and **monastic affairs (how to live and decide together)**.

- **Uposatha** — assembly every half month (on the fourteenth or fifteenth day) to recite the Prātimokṣa (the precepts), each monk confessing his own offences. This is the community's regular mechanism of self-purification, and the means by which the precepts are remembered and enforced in common;
- **The rains retreat** (varṣā) — during the three months of the rainy season (in China, from the fifteenth of the fourth month to the fifteenth of the seventh month) one does not wander, but dwells in one place for practice, avoiding the trampling of plants, insects, and ants and avoiding breaches of decorum through travelling; the retreat ends with the **Pravāraṇā**, at which the monks are invited to point out one another's faults;
- **Karma** — the procedure by which the community deliberates and decides, in three forms: the single motion, the motion with one resolution, and the motion with three resolutions (one announcement up to three announcements plus a vote), used for ordination, confession, the assignment of lodgings, the settlement of disputes, and every other monastic affair. **Every karma requires a "pure community" and a quorum; if the procedure is not in order, the matter does not stand.**

The significance of this level is that it shows the Buddhist precepts to be not a list of individual ethics but a set of **communal institutions** — enforceable, traceable, and repairable. The Vinaya collections of the Theravāda and the Sarvāstivāda preserve the most complete records of the procedures of these three observances; the *Dharmaguptaka Vinaya*, on which the Chinese Vinaya school relies, belongs to the Dharmaguptaka system.

The two pillars of Buddhist practice; every method of meditation can be assigned to one of the two or to their combination:

- **Calming (śamatha)** — settling the mind and abiding one-pointedly, accomplishing "one-pointedness of mind"; it belongs to [[samadhi_training|the training in concentration]];
- **Insight (vipaśyanā)** — observing dharmas in accordance with reality with wisdom (impermanence, suffering, [[anatta|non-self]]); it belongs to the training in wisdom.

**The relation between the two is the central question of all meditation theory**: concentration can suppress defilements but cannot cut them off, and the cutting off of defilements depends on insight; but if insight lacks concentration as its support, it is like a lamp in the wind, unable to illumine anything. Hence the śāstras say, "relying on calming, cultivate insight" — first collect the mind by calming, then break clinging by insight.

The schools differ in their emphasis on the two, and this determines the branching of the paths of practice:

- **The Southern tradition (*Visuddhimagga*)** — first cultivate calming through the forty objects of meditation to access concentration or absorption, then turn to insight, in a strict sequence, with sixteen stages of insight knowledge;
- **The Chinese Tiantai school (Zhiyi, *Mohe Zhiguan*)** — establishes the "dual cultivation of calming and insight" and "threefold contemplation in one mind," contemplating the present single moment of mind as at once empty, provisional, and the middle; calming and insight are not divided into before and after;
- **Yogācāra** — takes calming as the support of insight; the stage of preparation among the five stages is precisely the reliance on calming and insight to produce the four investigations and the four knowledges in accordance with reality;
- **Chan** — establishes no sequence and points directly at the present single moment of mind, but in the two modes of "silent illumination" (Caodong) and the "critical phrase" (Linji) it preserves a different emphasis on calming and insight.

**The dispute over "sudden awakening and gradual cultivation" has its point of issue here**: if calming and insight must be sequential, awakening must be gradual; if insight into the present single moment of mind already possesses the three truths, awakening can be sudden. Tiantai's classification of "sudden and gradual" as one of the four methods of conversion is precisely its adjudication of this question.

Comparison of the Pali Nikāyas with the Chinese Āgamas, Sanskrit and Gāndhārī fragments, the Vinayas of the schools, the [[ashoka|Aśoka]] stone edicts, and the analysis of textual stratification. Its conclusion is that there is no pure "original Buddhism", only a "[[early_layer|relatively early layer]]".

The method consists chiefly of the following:
- North–South comparison: the Pali *Saṃyutta Nikāya*, *Majjhima Nikāya*, *Dīgha Nikāya*, and *Aṅguttara Nikāya* and the Chinese *Saṃyukta-āgama*, *Madhyama-āgama*, *Dīrgha-āgama*, and *Ekottarika-āgama* belong to the recensions of different schools (*Saṃyukta-āgama* to the Sarvāstivāda recension, the Pali to the Vibhajyavāda); material common to both transmissions is generally regarded as earlier;
- Manuscripts and fragments: Sanskrit fragments and Gāndhārī manuscripts (unearthed in Afghanistan and Pakistan in modern times), which can go beyond the framework of the Chinese and Pali transmissions;
- Comparison of the Vinayas: corresponding passages in the extensive Vinayas of the various schools allow the layers of compilation to be identified;
- Inscriptions and archaeology: the Aśokan stone edicts (ca. 3rd century BCE) are Buddhism's earliest material historical source; they do not mention the specific content of doctrines such as the Four Noble Truths or dependent origination, and are an important basis for judging "the shape of Buddhism in Aśoka's time";
- Analysis of textual stratification: within a single scripture, the layers of prose and verse, the insertion of stereotyped formulas (such as "Thus have I heard" and "At one time the Buddha was staying in"), and patterns of repetition can all indicate accumulation from different periods.

The significance of this conclusion lies not in refuting tradition but in providing criteria for judging "which contents can be traced back relatively early". It is therefore a "restrictive" conclusion: it does not say "what original Buddhism was", only "which elements are, relatively speaking, earlier".

The [[four_truths|Four Noble Truths]], the [[eightfold|noble eightfold path]], the twelve links of dependent origination, the five aggregates, non-self, dependent origination, the four establishments of mindfulness, and nirvāṇa in this present life.

"Relatively early" is subject to three qualifications:
- Relativity: it does not mean that these contents underwent no development, but that they have counterparts in both the northern and southern transmissions and can be understood without relying on later systems;
- Stratification is not excluded: the core content of the Four Noble Truths, for example, is earlier, but the complete form of "three turnings and twelve aspects" may belong to the systematization of the redactional period;
- Form of teaching: the contents of this layer are mostly "analysis and practice" rather than "metaphysics" — they answer "how to observe, how to practice", not "what the world is".

The contents of this layer thus come closer to a combination of "empirical psychology + ethical practice + phenomenological analysis" than to a metaphysical system. This observation is also the reason the "minimal skeleton" can be maintained.

The Mahāyāna [[bodhisattva_path|bodhisattva path]], the Buddhas of the ten directions, the [[pure_land|Land of Bliss]], tathāgatagarbha, the Yogācāra and Madhyamaka systems, the tantras, and systems of doctrinal classification.

This layer is characterized by the addition of "expansion in time and space" and "ontological affirmation".

- Expansion: the horizon of early Buddhism centered on a single Buddha, Śākyamuni, and the present world; the developmental layer introduces the Buddhas of the ten directions, many Buddhas across many aeons, and pure lands in other directions (the Land of Bliss), and Buddhist cosmology unfolds from this;
- Affirmation: the early layer emphasizes the negative formulation of "non-self" and "absence of own-nature"; the developmental layer introduces affirmative formulations such as tathāgatagarbha and Buddha-nature, providing an ontological basis for the attainment of Buddhahood;
- Organization: Madhyamaka and Yogācāra established complete philosophical systems, while systems of doctrinal classification arranged the many scriptures into an order.

Chronologically, the Prajñāpāramitā sūtras (ca. 1st century BCE–2nd century CE) are the starting point of the developmental layer, followed in order by Madhyamaka (2nd–3rd century), the tathāgatagarbha scriptures (3rd–5th century), Yogācāra (4th–5th century), the tantras (after the 7th century), and the Chinese systems of doctrinal classification (6th–8th century).

Details of the [[six_realms|six realms]], hell tableaux, mechanized karmic retribution, rites for the deliverance of the dead, records of miraculous efficacy, and tales of karmic retribution. This layer serves a folk-religious function and differs in nature from the theoretical layer.

What "differs in nature" specifically means:
- The karmic fruition of the theoretical layer is a causal explanation that is "non-mechanical, non-fatalistic, and directed toward a specific remedy"; the karmic fruition of the folk layer often appears as "mechanically one-to-one retribution", whose function is moral exhortation and the maintenance of social order rather than a soteriology;
- The six realms of the theoretical layer are a classification of states of existence produced by karma; the six realms of the folk layer (especially the hells) develop into a pictorial system with specific names, locations, instruments of torture, and terms of punishment (hell tableaux), whose function is intimidation and consolation;
- The [[nirvana|nirvāṇa]] of the theoretical layer is "the cessation of suffering"; the folk layer's "deliverance of the dead" centers on the efficacy of ritual.

This layer is therefore not an "erroneous version" but a form of the same doctrine at another functional level. In theoretical exposition the two must be distinguished, otherwise one readily criticizes or defends the folk doctrine of mechanical karmic retribution as though it were Buddhism's official position — a confusion quite common in Buddhist studies.

If only the early, core, and verifiable contents are retained: suffering, the cause of suffering, the cessation of suffering, the path to the cessation of suffering, the [[eightfold|noble eightfold path]], the [[four_foundations|four establishments of mindfulness]], dependent origination, non-self, and nirvāṇa in this present life. This skeleton comes closer to a combination of "empirical psychology + ethical practice + phenomenological analysis".

The value of extracting a minimal skeleton lies not in "lightening the burden" of tradition but in identifying layers: by separating the "early core" from "later developments", one can see clearly which claims are non-negotiable for Buddhism and which are modes of organization that came with particular times and regions.

It should be noted that if this skeleton were taken as "true Buddhism" and put in place of tradition, several things would be lost:
- The principles of scriptural organization provided by doctrinal classification and the schools;
- Affirmative theories such as tathāgatagarbha and Buddha-nature that provide the basis for "why it is possible to attain Buddhahood";
- The altruistic motivation and limitless goal provided by the bodhisattva path;
- The complete system of precepts (if only the noble eightfold path is retained, the specific norms of the training in ethics cannot be established).

The approach taken on this page is to mark this skeleton as "identifiable content of the earlier layer" while retaining the complete structure of the developmental and folk layers — the layers are clearly distinguished, but no value judgment is pronounced.

The core teachings are simple, yet the scriptures are numerous; the reasons can be reduced to ten:

1. Oral transmission and the [[councils|councils]] required expansion, repetition, and classification — the mnemonic techniques of the oral age themselves produced large numbers of stereotyped formulas and repetitive structures;
2. The [[eighteen_schools|schisms of the schools]], each with its own transmission — the same scripture has different recensions in different schools (for example the *Saṃyukta-āgama* and the Pali *Saṃyutta Nikāya*);
3. Teaching according to capacity, with immeasurable methods — the same meaning is taught in several ways for different faculties;
4. The three baskets of sūtra, vinaya, and abhidharma differ in nature — sūtra is teaching, vinaya is normative rule, abhidharma is argumentation, and each has its own requirements of style and content;
5. Debate and doctrinal classification produced a great mass of treatises and commentaries — controversies among the schools and among the traditions continually generated new texts;
6. The later development of the Mahāyāna and the tantras — every turn in thought brought forth new scriptures;
7. Translation into many languages and regions — translation into the Sanskrit, Pali, Gāndhārī, Chinese, and Tibetan systems itself produced a great many texts;
8. The needs of folk-religious function — practices such as deliverance of the dead, prayer for blessing, and averting calamity required corresponding scriptures and ritual manuals;
9. The stages of practice and the details of the precepts must be elaborate — operable content cannot be simplified in its details;
10. Two thousand years of accumulation inevitably brought expansion.

In one sentence: the core is simple, the history is complex. The skeleton is simple; the flesh and blood are complex.

**The question**  The Mahāyāna scriptures (the Prajñāpāramitā, Lotus, Avataṃsaka, Pure Land, and others) appeared several centuries after the Buddha's demise; what is their relation to the early teachings?

**The traditional position**  The Mahāyāna sūtras are the Buddha's word, compiled in [[councils|councils]] by the assembly of bodhisattvas, kept in the nāga palace, or transmitted by Maitreya and Mañjuśrī — this is an answer internal to faith and cannot be used as a historical conclusion (equally, one cannot on this ground assert that the Mahāyāna is unrelated to early Buddhism).

**Several models in modern scholarship**

- **The Mahāsāṃghika-origins thesis** — the Mahāsāṃghika's open understanding of the Buddha-body and the [[bodhisattva_path|bodhisattva path]] as the proximate source of the Mahāyāna; this thesis has been very influential but lacks direct textual support and has largely been revised;
- **The stūpa-cult thesis** — the devotional forms of the Mahāyāna (worship of stūpas and relics and the wish to "see the Buddha") developed in parallel with the school-based monastic system, and the function of the Mahāyāna sūtras was precisely to provide doctrinal grounding for these communities of laypeople and mixed monastic-lay groups;
- **The forest-dwelling meditation thesis** — Mahāyāna prajñā and Madhyamaka thought originated in meditators' systematization of the contemplative state of "no reliance" (as in the Buddha-visualization of the *Pratyutpanna-buddha-saṃmukhāvasthita-samādhi-sūtra*);
- **The textual-stratification thesis** — the Mahāyāna sūtras are themselves products of long-term stratification, and the expansion of the Prajñāpāramitā from the *Aṣṭasāhasrikā* to the *Śatasāhasrikā* is direct evidence.

**Points that are relatively secure**  The Mahāyāna was not a unified sect but a **movement**; early Mahāyāna practitioners long lived in the same monasteries as the śrāvaka schools (as both the Chinese Vinayas and inscriptions attest); "Mahāyāna/Hīnayāna" as an identity distinction is a later development. The more prudent formulation is therefore that the Mahāyāna was an orientation long in gestation within early Buddhism (emphasizing prajñā, the bodhisattva path, and the cosmicization of the Buddha) that matured and took independent shape around the turn of the common era.

**Why this must be included in the account**  Without it, readers would understand the entire history of development through the binary of "the Buddha's word / not the Buddha's word", whereas in fact all the schools and the Mahāyāna were dealing with the same problem: **how non-self is compatible with karmic fruition and with the attainment of Buddhahood**.

**The Huichang persecution (845)**  Emperor Wuzong of Tang proscribed Buddhism, demolishing more than 4,600 temples and over 40,000 smaller shrines and retreats (zhaoti and lanruo), and forcing more than 260,000 monks and nuns to return to lay life. This was the largest persecution in Chinese Buddhist history (earlier those of Emperor Taiwu of the Northern Wei and Emperor Wu of the Northern Zhou, and later that of Emperor Shizong of the Later Zhou; together they are called "three Wu and one Zong").

**Why it determined the shape of the following millennium**  Different schools depended to different degrees on **forms of transmission**, and what was destroyed in the persecution was precisely what depended most on form:

- **[[tantra|Esoteric Buddhism]]** — dependent on the oral transmission of [[abhisheka|abhiṣeka]], maṇḍalas, and mudrās and mantras; once the lineage holders were scattered it could not be restored, and it died out in China proper (surviving in Japan);
- **The Faxiang, Jushe (Kośa), and Chengshi (Satyasiddhi) schools** — dependent on a great mass of treatises and commentaries and on scholarly monastic communities; after both the monasteries and the texts were destroyed they never recovered;
- **Chan** — holding to "no reliance on words" and practising "farming together with Chan" (the *Baizhang Qinggui*), it depended least on monastic economy and scripture, and so proved most resistant;
- **The Pure Land school** — simple in doctrine and extremely easy in practice (recitation of the Buddha's name), it did not depend on ritual or on the transmission of doctrine, and instead expanded among the people.

After the Song, the actual shape of Chinese Buddhism was "**dual practice of Chan and Pure Land**" together with the revival of the doctrinal schools (Tiantai and Huayan were restored when Song monks recovered lost commentaries from overseas). In the Yuan, Tibetan Buddhism entered China proper, and in the Ming and Qing the pattern of a confluence of Chan and Pure Land took shape. **To understand why present-day Chinese Buddhism is dominated by Chan and Pure Land, one must return to 845.**

Two changes in Buddhism in the modern era: **a change in research methods** and **the modernization of practice**.

**I. The methodological turn**  Since the 19th century, Buddhist studies has turned from apologetics and doctrinal learning toward philology and history: Sanskrit manuscripts (especially discoveries in Nepal and Central Asia), systematic collation of Pali texts with Chinese translations, inscriptional and art-historical evidence, and the recovery of **Gāndhārī manuscripts** (after the 1990s) have continually rewritten the basic chronology of Buddhist history — for example the dates of the Prajñāpāramitā and Mahāyāna sūtras have generally been pushed later, and the boundary between early Mahāyāna and the schools has become blurred. Today the "four-way collation of Sanskrit, Pali, Chinese, and Tibetan" is a standard method.

**II. The modernization of practice**

- **Humanistic Buddhism** — Taixu (1890–1947) proposed "Buddhism for human life", holding that Buddhism should attend to actual human life and society; developed by Yinshun, Hsing Yun, Sheng Yen, Cheng Yen, and others, it has become the most mainstream practical orientation in contemporary Chinese Buddhism;
- **The Buddhist revival and the lay movement** — Yang Wenhui (1837–1911) founded the Jinling Sutra Publishing House and recovered lost commentaries from Japan, promoting the modern Buddhist revival; the China Institute of Inner Learning (Zhina Neixueyuan) of Ouyang Jingwu and Lü Cheng initiated rigorous doctrinal scholarship;
- **Engaged Buddhism** — the this-worldly practice promoted in Vietnam and the West by Thich Nhat Hanh and others;
- **Buddhist modernism** — since the 20th century, the [[southern_tradition|southern tradition]], Chinese Buddhism, and Tibetan Buddhism have all produced forms that engage in dialogue with science and psychology ([[right_mindfulness|mindfulness]]), of which the secularization of mindfulness is the most conspicuous example.

**What this section is for**  It answers "where this two-thousand-year system stands today": the doctrinal forms remain, but the vehicles, methodology, and points of concern have changed. For readers engaged in systematic exposition, clearly distinguishing "Buddhism in history", "self-description within the tradition", and "modern scholarly reconstruction" is the precondition for avoiding the conflation of the three.

Hermeneutical principles that Buddhism itself provides, for deciding what to rely on when "the scriptures are many and their meanings divergent, and the teachers' explanations do not agree". The standard formulation comes from the *[[doc_7|Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra]]*:

1. **Rely on the Dharma, not on the person** — take the teaching itself as the standard, not the status of the speaker (this article leads Buddhists to be reserved about "the authority of lineage");
2. **Rely on the meaning, not on the words** — take the meaning referred to, not clinging to the wording that expresses it (this article opens the legitimacy of free translation and interpretation, and is also why [[yichang|matching meanings (ko-i)]] in Chinese translation could be accepted);
3. **Rely on the sūtras of definitive meaning, not on those of provisional meaning** — take the ultimate teaching as the standard and the expedient teaching as auxiliary (this article makes doctrinal classification necessary);
4. **Rely on wisdom, not on consciousness** — take wisdom that accords with reality as the standard, not discriminating consciousness (this article places the final criterion of interpretation in realization).

**Why these four deserve separate treatment**  They stand in exact contrast to the "method of textual stratification" in the twelfth layer of this page: the four reliances are **criteria internal to the tradition** (how to choose within the teaching), while the philological method is **the criterion of an external observer** (when, by whom, and on the basis of what materials this text was formed). Virtually every doctrinal classification and every sectarian controversy in Buddhist history cites one or another of the four reliances to endorse its own position — the controversy over "relying on the definitive meaning", for instance, is the controversy over whether tathāgatagarbha is definitive, while "relying on the meaning, not on the words" is the ground on which Chinese Buddhism dared to restate Indian thought in Chinese.

Distinguishing these two sets of criteria is the most practical form of self-protection in reading Buddhist literature.
